  <section class="wrap fiche-body">
    <div>
      <h2>The vibe</h2>
      <p>From Cabasson beach, the coastal path runs along the coast opposite Fort de Brégançon, between beaches, coves and pine woods. This is the "Plage de Cabasson – Plage du Pellegrin" section: 7.7 km in a classified site. The town specifies that the coastal path, an easement of 14.7 km in total, has two sections open to the public and that the Cabasson to Pellegrin one starts from Cabasson beach; the section linking it to the Favière path is closed by municipal order for safety reasons. Picnic tables are found at Cabasson, at the Estagnol and at Pellegrin (tourist office).</p>

      <h2>What to know before you go</h2>
      <p>According to the FAQ of the Bormes-les-Mimosas tourist office, dogs are accepted on hikes on a leash. Beaches have their own rules: from 15 June to 30 September, the town allows leashed dogs only on the part of <a href="/en/fiche-plage-cabasson-bormes-les-mimosas">Cabasson beach</a> near the Fort (east side of the rocky spur) and on Gouron beach, the other municipal beaches being reserved for working dogs. See also the <a href="/en/fiche-bormes-les-mimosas">Fort de Brégançon beach</a>. At the Estagnol, pets are strictly prohibited inside the property (paid park): listings of Provence-Alpes-Côte d'Azur Tourisme and the Bormes tourist office. Out of season, the tourist office says this beach can only be reached on foot via the coastal path, from Cabasson or from the Argentière beach (La Londe-les-Maures).</p>
      <p>For parking, the Cabasson beach car park is paid from 1 April to 1 November: €11 per car, €10 if booked at the tourist office from April to September, €8 from 5:30pm (tourist office 2026 fares). The Estagnol park, accessible by road from April to October from 8:30am to 7pm (8am to 8pm in July-August), charges €12 per car. Outside April to October, only the Cabasson car park is open and it is free.</p>
      <p>The commune's paths cross pastoral areas: the tourist office asks that if you meet a flock guarded by protection dogs, you keep your dog on a leash, do not stare the flock's dogs in the eyes and move away calmly. The town also reminds visitors that waste must be picked up on the permitted beaches.</p>
      <div class="callout">Coco's tip: in summer, check the beach area allowed for dogs before parking, and keep your dog on a leash along the whole path.</div>

      <h2>Nearby</h2>
      <p>Fort de Brégançon dominates the landscape. The small Brégançon beach, next to the fort on the left of Cabasson beach when facing the sea, welcomes dogs year-round according to the tourist office. The office also specifies that dogs are not admitted in the town's parks, that the historic guided tour of the village can be done with your dog, and lists a veterinary clinic (Les Mimosas) in the commune.</p>
    </div>

